 To solve this, we first condense the right-hand side to a common denominator.
\begin{align*} \frac{A}{x - 2} + \frac{B}{x - 3} + \frac{C}{x - 5} &= \frac{A(x - 3)(x - 5)}{(x - 2)(x - 3)(x - 5)} + \frac{B(x - 2)(x - 5)}{(x - 2)(x - 3)(x - 5)} + \frac{C(x - 2)(x - 3)}{(x - 2)(x - 3)(x - 5)} \\ &= \frac{A(x - 3)(x - 5) + B(x - 2)(x - 5) + C(x - 2)(x - 3)}{(x - 2)(x - 3)(x - 5)}. \end{align*}
After multiplying out the numerator, we get \[A(x - 3)(x - 5) + B(x - 2)(x - 5) + C(x - 2)(x - 3) = A(x^2 - 8x + 15) + B(x^2 - 7x + 10) + C(x^2 - 5x + 6).\]The right-hand side factors to \begin{align*} A(x - 6 + \tfrac{1}{x - 6}) + B(x - 4 + \tfrac{1}{x - 4}) + C(x - 6 + \tfrac{1}{x - 6}) \\ \qquad= \tfrac{A}{6-x} + B \cdot \tfrac{1}{4-x} + C \cdot \tfrac{1}{2-x}.\end{align*}Setting $x = 2,$ the second term does not exist, but the first and third are both defined, so \[3 = \tfrac{A}{3} + \tfrac{C}{1}.\]Set $x = 3$ and we have \[2 = \tfrac{A}{3} + \tfrac{2B}{1} + \tfrac{C}{2}.\]We have already solved $A + 3C = 3,$ so substitute $A = 3 - 3C$ to get \[2 = \tfrac{3 - 3C}{3} + 2B + \tfrac{C}{2} = -C + 2B.\]Now set $x = 5$ giving \[4 = \tfrac{A}{-3} + \tfrac{B}{-1} + \tfrac{2C}{1}.\]We now have the system \[\begin{cases} A + 3C = 3 \\ -C + 2B = 2 \\ -\tfrac{A}{3} - B + 2C = 4. \end{cases}\]We have $A = 3 - 3C$ and substitute into the third equation to get \[-\tfrac{3 - 3C}{3} - B + 2C = 4 \Rightarrow -1 + C - B + 2C = 4 \Rightarrow 3C - B = 5 \Rightarrow B=3C-5.\]Substitute this into the second equation to get \[-C + 2(3C - 5) = 2 \Rightarrow -C + 6C - 10 = 2 \Rightarrow 5C = 12 \Rightarrow C = \frac{12}{5},\]so $B = 3 \cdot \frac{12}{5} - 5 = \frac{11}{5}.$ Substituting these into $A = 3 - 3C$ we have $A = 3 - 3 \cdot \frac{12}{5} = -\frac{21}{5}.$ Finally, we have $(A,B,C) = \boxed{\left(-\frac{21}{5}, \frac{11}{5}, \frac{12}{5}\right)}.$ </think> <answer>(-21/5, 11/5, 12/5)</answer>
